  "query_preview": "..Southwesterly winds will remain elevated on Friday\nwith similar wind gust magnitudes to today (30-45 mph) although a\nbit more expansive area of these higher gusts. 700 mb winds\nmaximize on Friday morning before gradually decreasing during the\nday which will help to prevent the winds from gusting higher and\nfor as long. Even though a few areas will see gusts to advisory\nlevels the duration and spatial extent should be limited. \n\nThe next chance for precipitation arrives on Sunday as moisture\nfrom Hurricane Marie is advected into the region as a trough in\nthe PNW rotates a jet max into Utah on Sunday. The right entrance\nregion of this jet will lead to upper-level divergence and\nwidespread precipitation. The biggest question will be how quickly\nthe base of this trough rotates through. Ahead of the trough will\nbe relatively warm and moist air while behind it will be drier and\ncooler air. If it arrives faster than the moisture then\nprecipitation will be further east. This is evident amongst the\nensemble members as PWATs for KSLC range from 0.7\" to 1.00\" on\nSunday. QPF totals have come down a bit as the arrival of\nprecipitation looks more like an early to mid morning event with\ncloud cover limiting instability and higher rainfall rates.\n\nRidging will build back into Utah and SW Wyoming for the first\nhalf of next week with seasonal temperatures. The moisture from\nHurricane Marie could make another appearance as Marie decays over\nthe Pacific and the remnant moisture is pulled into the west\nthanks to the PNW trough.",
